<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Schiller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a>–<a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich von Schiller</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Schiller was a German poet, dramatist, historian and philosopher. His first play, The Robbers (1781), was followed by Don Carlos, the Wallenstein trilogy, Mary Stuart and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a>. He taught history at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> from 1789 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1799, where his friendship with Goethe defined Weimar Classicism. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a> was set by Beethoven in the Ninth Symphony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller.jpg" alt="Friedrich Schiller" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Graff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_Graff_-_Friedrich_Schiller.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the German Sturm und Drang, “storm and stress”, of the 1770s, young writers rebelled against rules in the name of passion and genius: Johann Wolfgang von Goethe’s The Sorrows of Young Werther of 1774 moved a generation of readers, and <b>Friedrich Schiller</b>’s The Robbers of 1781 shook audiences.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Schiller</b> wrote &quot;An die Freude&quot; in the summer of 1785.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Duchess Anna Amalia and Charles Augustus the united duchy became the home of Goethe and <b>Schiller</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar</a></li><li><blockquote>Duchess Anna Amalia, regent from 1758, invited Christoph Martin Wieland as tutor; her son Charles Augustus brought Goethe to Weimar in 1775, later followed by Herder and <b>Schiller</b>, while the University of Jena flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Schiller</b> first met Goethe in Rudolstadt in 1788.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-rudolstadt">Principality of Schwarzburg-Rudolstadt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Marbach am Neckar, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.94,9.26&amp;year=1788" aria-label="Marbach am Neckar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 455 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Friedrich Schiller"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.94,9.26&amp;year=1788">What was here in 1788? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yes! there's a limit to the despot's power! When the oppressed looks round in vain for justice, When his sore burden may no more be borne, With fearless heart he makes appeal to Heaven, And thence brings down his everlasting rights…&#8221;</p><footer>Wilhelm Tell (1804), act 2, scene 2 &#183; tr. Theodore Martin, 1847 &#183; spoken by Stauffacher at the Rütli &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6788/pg6788-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But the strong man is the strongest when alone.&#8221;</p><footer>Wilhelm Tell (1804), act 1, scene 3 &#183; tr. Theodore Martin, 1847 &#183; spoken by Tell &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6788/pg6788-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…man only plays when in the full meaning of the word he is a man, and he is only completely a man when he plays.&#8221;</p><footer>Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man (1795), letter 15 &#183; anonymous translation (Aesthetical Essays, Bohn edition, 19th century) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6798/pg6798-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;One pen-stroke now, One motion of your hand, can new create The earth! but grant us liberty of thought.&#8221;</p><footer>Don Carlos (1787), act 3 &#183; tr. Richard Dillon Boylan &#183; spoken by the Marquis of Posa to King Philip II &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6789/pg6789-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
